{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 8394 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "403480e40b8020f10fe8ec46f90c1c61e5c314552df1288669d1c4ad37f4e365" :edb/db-before-t 8393 :edb/db-after-t 8394 :edb/tx-data [[13194139541706 50 #inst "2026-10-26T22:13:26.246Z" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115049 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }-3{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}=-3\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y = \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x.$\n\n![](images/07/074a330c55b63458fe94ed36b0c569ecfc6ce8fd839b8566f1dd589440c59c69.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = \\infty.\n$$\n\nHowever, the negative factor $-3$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = -\\infty.\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115049 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-3{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}=-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y = \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x.$\n\n![](images/07/074a330c55b63458fe94ed36b0c569ecfc6ce8fd839b8566f1dd589440c59c69.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = \\infty.\n$$\n\nHowever, the negative factor $-3$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = -\\infty.\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186115061 1071 "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} (3^{2x} + 2^{3x} + 1).\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186115061 1071 "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 1} (3^{2x} + 2^{3x} + 1).\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115061 1074 "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}({3}^{2x}+{2}^{3x}+1) & ={3}^{2(1)}+{2}^{3(1)}+1 \\\\  & =9+8+1 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115061 1074 "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}({3}^{2x}+{2}^{3x}+1) & ={3}^{2(1)}+{2}^{3(1)}+1 \\\\  & =9+8+1 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186115069 1071 "Compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} -5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{1+x}.\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186115069 1071 "Compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to \\infty} -5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{1+x}.\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115069 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }-5{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1+x} & =-5\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1+x} \\\\  & =-5\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x} \\\\  & =-5\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y = \\left(\\dfrac 1 3\\right)^x.$\n\n![](images/81/81be7f2983aeb647a8fa88e2caad8cea3c2052190222670838077e760a2e7fda.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}   \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = 0,\n$$\n\nand finally, we have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{5}{3}\\cdot \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}   \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = -\\dfrac 5 3\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115069 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-5{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1+x} & =-5{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1+x} \\\\  & =-5{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y = \\left(\\dfrac 1 3\\right)^x.$\n\n![](images/81/81be7f2983aeb647a8fa88e2caad8cea3c2052190222670838077e760a2e7fda.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty}   \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = 0,\n$$\n\nand finally, we have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{5}{3}\\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty}   \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = -\\dfrac 5 3\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186115816 1071 "Find \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{4^{x} - 5}{2^{x} - 2}\n$$\n\n." 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115816 1071 "Find $lim_(x → 0)\\frac{4^{x} - 5}{2^{x} - 2}$." 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186115878 1071 "Find \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to  3}10^{3x - 7}\n$$\n\n." 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115878 1071 "Find $lim_(x → 3)10^{3x - 7}$." 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186115879 1071 "What is \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}2^{2x + 4}\n$$\n\n?" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115879 1071 "What is $lim_(x → ∞)2^{2x + 4}$?" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186115880 1071 "What is \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}8^{-x}\n$$\n\n?" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115880 1071 "What is $lim_(x → ∞)8^{-x}$?" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186115881 1071 "\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3x+1)}=\n$$\n\n" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115881 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3x+1)}=$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186115881 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3x+1)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\cdot e \\\\  & =e\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={e}^{-3x}:$\n\n![](images/c3/c3d9e0bfa0b7514713c01289fc63fde7b0bd0dab525ad50617a9e908fd117060.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\ne\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}=e\\cdot 0=0\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115881 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3x+1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\cdot e \\\\  & =e\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={e}^{-3x}:$\n\n![](images/c3/c3d9e0bfa0b7514713c01289fc63fde7b0bd0dab525ad50617a9e908fd117060.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\ne\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}=e\\cdot 0=0\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186115902 1071 "What is \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}(-7 \\cdot 6^{x})\n$$\n\n?" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186115902 1071 "What is $lim_(x → - ∞)(-7 \\cdot 6^{x})$?" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186235521 1071 "For the function $\\widehat{T}(t)$ from the previous part, what is \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\widehat{T}(t)?\n$$\n\n\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\widehat{T}(t)\\,=\\,\n$$\n\n\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186235521 1071 "For the function $\\widehat{T}(t)$ from the previous part, what is ${lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\widehat{T}(t)?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n${lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\widehat{T}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186235521 1074 "From the previous part, we know that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{T}(t)=25+3{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\widehat{T}(t) & =\\lim_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(25+3{e}^{-0.03t}) \\\\  & =25+3\\lim_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }{e}^{-0.03t} \\\\  & =25+3\\,{e}^{\\,\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(-0.03t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{\\,\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(-0.03t)}=\\infty ,$ and the positive factor of $3$ does not affect the sign of the limit, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to -\\,\\infty }\\widehat{T}(t)=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186235521 1074 "From the previous part, we know that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{T}(t)=25+3{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\widehat{T}(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(25+3{e}^{-0.03t}) \\\\  & =25+3{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }{e}^{-0.03t} \\\\  & =25+3\\,{e}^{\\,\\,{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(-0.03t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{\\,\\,{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(-0.03t)}=\\infty ,$ and the positive factor of $3$ does not affect the sign of the limit, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -\\,\\infty }\\widehat{T}(t)=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186297276 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot {5}^{x})=\n$$\n\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186297276 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot {5}^{x})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186297276 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot {5}^{x})=2\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={5}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/02/02d46e13c84fc575f489a9870f3b1213eb3d180f3f5ef6e6a546d3acfe4b2282.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound  as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=2\\cdot \\infty =\\infty .\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186297276 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot {5}^{x})=2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={5}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/02/02d46e13c84fc575f489a9870f3b1213eb3d180f3f5ef6e6a546d3acfe4b2282.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound  as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=2\\cdot \\infty =\\infty .\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186314748 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{5}^{x}+c}{{2}^{x}-1}=4,\n$$\n\n what is the value of constant $c?$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186314748 1071 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{5}^{x}+c}{{2}^{x}-1}=4,$ what is the value of constant $c?$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186314748 1074 "We can evaluate this limit in terms of the constant $c$ using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{5}^{x}+c}{{2}^{x}-1} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 1}({5}^{x}+c)}{\\lim_{x\\to 1}({2}^{x}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{1}+c}{{2}^{1}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+c}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+c}{1} \\\\  & =5+c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the above limit is equal to $4,$ we can equate the above with $4,$ and then solve for $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+c & =4 \\\\ c & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186314748 1074 "We can evaluate this limit in terms of the constant $c$ using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{5}^{x}+c}{{2}^{x}-1} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}({5}^{x}+c)}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}({2}^{x}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{1}+c}{{2}^{1}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+c}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+c}{1} \\\\  & =5+c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the above limit is equal to $4,$ we can equate the above with $4,$ and then solve for $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+c & =4 \\\\ c & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186314749 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}{4}^{x/2}=\n$$\n\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186314749 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}{4}^{x/2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186314749 1074 "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}{4}^{x/2} & ={4}^{2/2} \\\\  & ={4}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186314749 1074 "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{4}^{x/2} & ={4}^{2/2} \\\\  & ={4}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186314750 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{3}^{x}+5}{{2}^{x}-3}=\n$$\n\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186314750 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{3}^{x}+5}{{2}^{x}-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186314750 1074 "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{3}^{x}+5}{{2}^{x}-3} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 2}({3}^{x}+5)}{\\lim_{x\\to 2}({2}^{x}-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}+5}{{2}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+5}{4-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{1} \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 true] [17592186314750 1074 "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{3}^{x}+5}{{2}^{x}-3} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({3}^{x}+5)}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({2}^{x}-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}+5}{{2}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+5}{4-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{1} \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541706 17592186576411 false] [17592186314751 1071 "Evaluate the followi